==== Subportals ====
Portals may recurse into narrower reader-facing surfaces when a field becomes too broad for one useful entry point.

Examples:
* [[Portal:Biology]] → [[Portal:Biology/Bioinformatics|Bioinformatics]]
* [[Portal:Computer science]] → [[Portal:Computer science/Python|Python]]

A subportal is a '''navigation relationship''', not ontology inheritance. A child portal may represent a subfield, method, language, technology, interdisciplinary surface, or other coherent way into the corpus. It does not become an Entity type or Domain merely because it is nested beneath another portal.

Portal paths should favor progressive disclosure: simple orientation first, then core concepts, then technical depth. This lets a novice and an expert enter the same knowledge surface at different levels without creating separate competing taxonomies.

=== Indices ===
Large-scale maps and registries organized for lookup.

=== Outlines ===
Hierarchical decompositions used to understand the internal structure of a field without claiming the outline is the ontology itself.

=== Contents pages ===
Broad human-readable directories across major knowledge surfaces.

=== Categories ===
Human-readable classification and browsing groups.

=== Semantic queries and knowledge graphs ===
Computed/visual views over structured properties and relationships. Graphs are views over knowledge, not independent truth.

== Systems science orientation ==
Historical design placed Systems Science / Systems Theory as the top or root priority: individual knowledge domains are interpreted as interacting systems rather than isolated silos. This is an intellectual orientation, not a demand that every page literally be nested beneath one category.

